nt_id` as processed, so a replayed event went through again and was subtracted from `balance_due_cents` a second time. It now saves the ID, and a replay returns `applied: False, reason: 'duplicate'` with the balance unchanged.\n\n2. **`invoice.payment_failed`:** this now works the same way as the other handlers. It calculates `fee_cents` with `compute_fee_cents`, adds `amount_cents + fee_cents` to the balance, adds 1 to `failed_attempts`, sets the status to `past_due`, updates `last_event_at`, records the event ID and returns the usual result dictionary. For the test event ($10.00 at a 300 bps fee) that gives a 30-cent fee and a $10.30 balance, and a retry is treated as a duplicate.\n\n3. **Cleanup:**\n   - **Removed** the two commented-out drafts: an old float-based fee function and an early `payment_failed` handler. Both used float math that rounded down instead of half-up.\n   - **Removed** the comments that just repeated what the next line did.\n   - **Simplified:** the result dictionary was written out five times, so I moved it into one small `_result` helper.\n\n**Ordering check kept:** the comment above the check that rejects older events said it was obsolete and could be removed. I left the check in, because it is what stops an older event from overwriting newer subscription state, and replaced the comment with one that says so. Fees are still calculated before any state changes, so invalid input raises an error without partially updating the subscription.","ttft_ms":2334,"type":"result","duration_ms":25657,"uuid":"46cbe18f-bd56-446e-b25e-e8396318a247","ttft_stream_ms":997,"time_to_request_ms":305,"first_content_frame_ms":997,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-12-23T22:59:00.336Z"}

